+# Flashing from Linux
+
+bb10mt can flash BB10 devices from Linux without Windows, Wine or a VM. This was verified on a BlackBerry Leap STR100-2 (hardware ID `0x06002E0A`, bootrom 5.47.0.3) on 2 October 2026: stock 10.3.3.3216 OS and radio, then an AutoRoot v3 user image, both flashed with `bb10mt flash` from Arch Linux and both booted.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- libusb 1.0
+- FUSE 2 (`libfuse.so.2`). On Arch: `pacman -S fuse2`. On Debian/Ubuntu: `apt install libfuse2`. Without it bb10mt exits with `error while loading shared libraries: libfuse.so.2`.
+- `loaders.dat` and `bb10mt.ini` next to the binary.
+- USB access for your user, for example:
+
+  ```
+  echo 'SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0fca", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"' | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/51-blackberry.rules
+  sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger
+  ```
+
+## Entering the bootrom: start bb10mt first
+
+The bootrom only appears (`0fca:0001`) briefly at power-on, and only if a host is already polling. A device that is booted with USB set to mass storage (`0fca:8014`) exposes only a SCSI interface, so bb10mt cannot reboot it and waits on `Connecting to BootROM...`.
+
+1. Start the bb10mt command (`info` or `flash`).
+2. Unplug the device and power it fully off.
+3. Plug it in while off (tap power if nothing happens).
+
+If a session is aborted, the bootrom can be left unresponsive at `0fca:0001`. Unplug, hold power for 10 to 15 seconds, and catch it again.
+
+## Read-only check
+
+```
+./bb10mt info
+```
+
+This loads the RAM loader and prints the hardware ID, bootrom version, MCT/partition map, installed OS and blocklists, then reboots the device. It writes nothing. `No valid loader found for XXXXXXXX` means the hardware ID is missing from `[Loaders]` in `bb10mt.ini`.
+
+## Flashing a stock autoloader
+
+Split the autoloader into its payloads, then flash them in one session (OS first, then radio):
+
+```
+./bb10mt split -i Z30_10.3.3.3216_STA100.exe -o stock/
+./bb10mt flash -i stock/<os>.signed,stock/<radio>.signed
+```
+
+Stock payloads end in a genuine 560-byte `QNXH-OS-1` seal, which bb10mt sends as the signature trailer. On the Leap the 3 GB OS took about four minutes; the device then booted to the setup wizard.
+
+Official autoloaders carry 0x70 extra bytes between the end of the PE image and the payload table (signature `0x97C5D59C` repeated three times). Tools that parse the table should search for that signature, not assume it starts at the PE end.
+
+## Flashing an AutoRoot v3 user image
+
+`root_v3.sh` writes Windows `.exe` autoloaders, but on Linux you can flash its intermediate files directly. The device must already run the stock build the image was made from:
+
+```
+./bb10mt flash -i work/<name>.0.unsigned,stock/<radio>.signed
+```
+
+The user-partition image (packed with `pack -s`) was accepted and booted.
